\section{Cognitive social structures}{

A cognitive social structure records each node's report of the ties in
the whole network, in a \code{by} column that names who reported each tie.
Counting every report as a tie of its own would count each tie once for
every perceiver who reports it.
So the functions here first combine the reports into the locally
aggregated structure of Krackhardt (1987), with the intersection rule:
a tie exists if both of its ends report it, and a message says so.
A tie that names no reporter is kept as it is.

A tie-level function still returns one value for each report,
so that the result can be added back to the network it was given.
Each report takes the value of the tie that it reports.
A report of a tie that is not in the aggregated structure takes \code{NA},
or \code{FALSE} for a mark.
\code{tie_is_random()} is the exception, and draws among the reports.

To combine the reports in a different way, do this before the function,
e.g. with \code{manynet::to_aggregated(over = "by")}.

Krackhardt, David. 1987.
"Cognitive social structures".
\emph{Social Networks} 9(2): 109-134.
\doi{10.1016/0378-8733(87)90009-8}
}

\section{Cost of the enumerated measures}{

\code{net_by_cohesion()} and \code{net_by_adhesion()} are connectivity problems that
\code{{igraph}} solves directly, so they run on a network of any size.
\code{net_by_strength()} and \code{net_by_toughness()} instead take a minimum over
every subset of the tieset or the nodeset, which is \eqn{2^n} subsets.
Their cost therefore quadruples for every two nodes added: a ring of 16
nodes takes about 8 seconds, one of 20 over two minutes, and one of 30
more than a day. \code{limit} stops each before it becomes a hang.
}

\section{Weighted networks}{

\code{net_by_adhesion()} reads a tie's weight as its capacity,
and so returns the smallest total weight of ties whose removal would
increase the number of components, the network's minimum cut,
rather than the smallest number of such ties.
This is also the smallest maximum flow between any two of its nodes;
see \code{\link[=node_by_flow]{node_by_flow()}} for the flow between particular nodes.
Use \code{\link[manynet:to_unweighted]{manynet::to_unweighted()}} first to count ties instead.
The other measures documented here count nodes or ties whatever
their weight.
}

\section{Signed networks}{

\code{net_by_adhesion()} asks what holds a network together,
and a negative tie is hostility rather than a channel along which
cohesion travels.
Where the network is signed, it therefore considers only the positive
ties, and says so.
Use \code{\link[manynet:to_unsigned]{manynet::to_unsigned()}} first to control this yourself.
The other measures documented here use every tie whatever its sign.
}

\section{Robustness}{

\code{net_by_robustness()} removes the nodes of a network one at a time,
recording after each removal the share of the original nodes that
remain in the largest weak component.
It returns the mean of these shares over all \eqn{N} removals,
Schneider et al.'s \eqn{R}.
A star attacked at its hub falls apart at once, scoring about \eqn{1/N},
while a complete network loses only one node with each removal,
scoring \eqn{(N-1)/2N}, which approaches the maximum of 0.5.
Comparing a random failure against an attack shows, as Albert, Jeong and
Barabási found, that a network with a few hubs tolerates failure
but is fragile under attack.

The share remaining after each number of removals is kept as the
\code{curve} attribute of the result, a table with columns
\code{removed}, the share of the nodes removed, and \code{giant},
the share of the original nodes in the largest component,
averaged over the \code{times} runs.
Retrieve it with \code{attr(x, "curve")}.

Nodes are chosen at random wherever several are tied,
so the result is averaged over \code{times} runs, by default 100.
Where an attack meets no ties, every run would be the same,
so it runs once.
Each run computes the measure once per node removed,
so an adaptive attack by an expensive measure, such as betweenness,
on a network of several hundred nodes can take some minutes.
Lower \code{times}, or attack with a \code{node_measure} object, to go faster.

For signed networks,
\code{net_by_robustness()} considers only the positive ties,
since a negative tie does not hold a component together.

For two-mode networks,
\code{\link[=node_is_max]{node_is_max()}} marks the highest-scoring node in each mode,
so an attack on a two-mode network chooses at random between the two.
}

